 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Hi, the light in my Neff oven has stopped working (model B1ACE4HN0B). Oven works fine otherwise. Can I change the bulb myself and what bulb do I need?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, you can change the bulb yourself; it is a standard owner-safe task. Neff ovens typically use a heat-resistant 300°C bulb with an SES (small Edison screw) fitting, often rated at 25W. To replace it, ensure the oven is switched off and cool, then remove any protective glass cover by unscrewing it counter-clockwise. Once the cover is off, you can usually twist the old bulb out and fit the new one. Please check if your bulb has a clear glass shield that needs removing first."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Thanks. I've isolated it at the cooker switch and let it cool, and got the glass cover off using a rubber glove. The old bulb says E14, 25W, 300C and the filament looks broken. Is E14 right, or do I need a different one?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, E14 is correct; that is the standard small screw fitting for Neff ovens. The bulb has simply blown. Fit a new 300°C heat-resistant E14 bulb (usually 25W or similar), ensuring the oven remains isolated while you do so. Once fitted, restore power and test it."
  }
 ],
